Month = {2026 SEP 18},
Abstract = {Climate policy attitudes are increasingly polarized across Western
   Europe. We examine how affective polarization shapes these attitudes,
   arguing that affective distance between opposing identity camps
   contributes to voters adopting climate positions that align with their
   own side and diverge from their opponents. In Western European
   multiparty systems, Green and right-wing populist parties serve as
   visible anchors of these camps, such that greater affective distance
   between them should be associated with more polarized climate attitudes,
   particularly where voters perceive the parties as clearly divided on
   climate policy. We test this argument in Germany between 2016 and 2025,
   focusing on the Greens and the AfD. Using German Longitudinal Election
   Study panel data, within-respondent fixed-effects models, and two-wave
   campaign analyses, we find that greater Green-AfD affective distance is
   associated with movement toward climate positions aligned with a
   respondent's side, especially as voters perceive larger differences
   between the parties on climate policy.},
