Month = {DEC 31},
Abstract = {Human-induced land use practices can result in the bioaccumulation of
   metals in turtles. We assessed relationships between land use and
   pollutant concentrations in painted turtles (Chrysemys picta) and
   snapping turtles (Chelydra serpentina) through non-destructive tissue
   sampling across 22 sites within a mixed-use watershed in north-central
   West Virginia, U.S.A.. Metal concentrations were consistently higher in
   nails than in blood. Selenium levels in soil and turtle nails were
   positively correlated with proximity to mine land. Lead levels in turtle
   nails were positively correlated with Pb concentrations in soil. All
   samples had Hg levels that exceeded consumption limits set by the United
   States Environmental Protection Agency.},
